Q: Convert jpg to webp using imagewebp A: <h2>Converting JPG to WEBP is as simple as that</h2>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$image = imagecreatefromjpeg('example.jpg');
imagewebp($image, 'example.webp');
imagedestroy($image);
</code></pre>
<p>This creates an &quot;example.webp&quot; file from an &quot;example.jpg&quot;. You can <a href="https://www.w3schools.com/php/func_filesystem_unlink.asp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">delete the JPG file</a> after.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>There's nothing wrong with the code in the question - the OP encountered a <strong>bug</strong>, which he <a href="https://bugs.php.net/bug.php?id=70045" rel="nofollow noreferrer">reported</a> and <a href="https://bugs.php.net/bug.php?id=66590" rel="nofollow noreferrer">got taken care of</a> <strong>in PHP 5.6.11. Later versions have this bug fixed.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Converting various formats to WEBP</h2>
<p>This function converts files of common image formats (<code>PNG</code>, <code>JPG/JPEG</code>, <code>GIF</code>, <code>WEBP</code>) to <code>WEBP</code>.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/language.types.declarations.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Type declarations</a> require at least PHP 7.1.0. Remove them if your version is prior.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>/**
 * @param string $inputFile: relative or absolute path
 * @param string $outputFile: relative or absolute path
 * @param int $quality of output: 0 is worst, 100 is best
 * @return void
 */
function convertImageToWebp(string $inputFile, string $outputFile, int $quality = 100): void
{
    $fileType = exif_imagetype($inputFile);

    switch ($fileType) {
        case IMAGETYPE_GIF:
            $image = imagecreatefromgif($inputFile);
            imagepalettetotruecolor($image);
            imagealphablending($image, true);
            imagesavealpha($image, true);
            break;
        case IMAGETYPE_JPEG:
            $image = imagecreatefromjpeg($inputFile);
            break;
        case IMAGETYPE_PNG:
            $image = imagecreatefrompng($inputFile);
            imagepalettetotruecolor($image);
            imagealphablending($image, true);
            imagesavealpha($image, true);
            break;
        case IMAGETYPE_WEBP:
            rename($inputFile, $outputFile);
            return;
        default:
            return;
    }

    imagewebp($image, $outputFile, $quality);

    imagedestroy($image);
}
</code></pre>
<h4>Usage examples:</h4>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>convertImageToWebp('image.png', 'image.webp');
convertImageToWebp('/var/www/app/assets/image.jpg', '/var/www/app/assets/image.webp');
convertImageToWebp('/home/paul/image.gif', 'image.webp', 90);
</code></pre>
<p>Both relative and absolute paths are fine. The 3rd quality param is optional - I've set a default.</p>
<h4>Requirements:</h4>
<p>Add this to your <code>composer.json</code> under <code>require</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>&quot;ext-exif&quot;: &quot;*&quot;,
&quot;ext-gd&quot;: &quot;*&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>The <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/intro.exif.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>exif</code> extension</a> is needed for the <code>exif_imagetype</code> function.<br>The <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/ref.image.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>gd</code> extension</a> is there for the other ones: <code>imagecreatefromxyz</code>, <code>imagewebp</code>, <code>imagedestroy</code>.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>These lines in the <code>composer.json</code> just ensure the two extensions are present. Both must be installed and enabled on your system, already. If you're using the <code>php:8.4-fpm</code> Docker image like me, I've got an example at the bottom of <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79890276/20594090">this other answer</a>.</p>
</blockquote>
<h4>Explanation:</h4>
<ul>
<li><p>the <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.exif-imagetype.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>exif_imagetype</code> function</a> reads the file type from the image's metadata</p>
</li>
<li><p>if the <code>$inputFile</code> is</p>
<ul>
<li>a <code>WEBP</code> already: it is just moved to the <code>$outputFile</code> location</li>
<li>a <code>PNG</code>/<code>JPG/JPEG</code> or <code>GIF</code>: the image is
<ol>
<li>loaded via <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.imagecreatefromjpeg.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">imagecreatefromxyz</a>,</li>
<li>converted and written to the <code>$outputFile</code> with the <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.imagewebp.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">imagewebp function</a> (yes, <strong>it both converts and writes</strong>) and</li>
<li>finally destroyed by <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.imagedestroy.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">imagedestroy</a> (the image object, to free up memory)</li>
</ol>
</li>
<li>something else: just exit</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><p>if the <code>$outputFile</code> already exists, it will be overwritten (both <code>rename</code> and <code>imagewebp</code> functions do so)</p>
</li>
<li><p>the 3 extra lines for the <code>GIF</code> and the <code>PNG</code> are required because of their special characteristics (color representation, transparency)</p>
</li>
</ul>
